Thanks for the report! I've included a workaround below that I think gives the info you need. (We'll have to think more deeply about changing the built-in reporter as some folks may already depend on the format of it's output in, for example, CI/CD reporting. But hopefully this helps in the interim!)

**Let me know if the below unblocks you!**

With the workaround, the output looks like:

```
$ npx playwright test --reporter=line,./DetailedSkipInfoReporter.ts

Running 6 tests using 6 workers

  6 skipped

  Skipped Breakdown: 3 skipped, 3 fixme
```

To get this output (the additional line with _Skipped Breakdown_), you can do the following:

1. Save/check-in the [Custom Reporter](https://playwright.dev/docs/test-reporters#custom-reporters) I've included at the end of this comment called `DetailedSkipInfoReporter.ts`.
2. Add the custom reporter at the end of the reporters array in your playwright config (or on the command line like `npx playwright test --reporter=line,./DetailedSkipInfoReporter.ts`)

```ts
// @filename: DetailedSkipInfoReporter.ts
import type { FullConfig, FullResult, Reporter, Suite } from '@playwright/test/reporter';

class DetailedSkipInfoReporter implements Reporter {
  private _suite!: Suite;

  onBegin(config: FullConfig, suite: Suite) {
    this._suite = suite;
  }

  onEnd(result: FullResult) {
    let fixme = 0;
    let skip = 0;
    for (const test of this._suite.allTests()) {
      if (test.expectedStatus === 'skipped') {
        if (test.annotations.some(a => a.type === 'fixme')) {
          fixme++;
        } else {
          skip++;
        }
      }
    }

    console.log(`\n  Skipped Breakdown: ${skip} skipped, ${fixme} fixme`);
  }
}

export default DetailedSkipInfoReporter;
```

As an aside: test.fixme is effectively the same as test.skip, except for the annotation type. Some reporters, like the HTML Reporter, will show you the fixme vs. skip annotation(s) when you click on a test to view it.

